{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775344028-y32Hv20YnlVnHtWvn05n","role":"assistant","created":1775344044,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The parsing is challenging because of the HTML table structure. Let me take a different approach - I'll manually read the totals from each file by looking at the data I already captured.\n\nFrom the 1972 file (treasury_bulletin_1972_03_page_45.txt):\nLooking at the table, the \"Final maturity\" column shows the year when securities will mature. I can see:\n- For the row labeled \"1976\" (final maturity), there's a \"Total\" on the right side showing 14,731\n- The left side shows securities maturing in 1972\n\nWait, I think I was misreading the table structure. Let me look more carefully at the data I captured earlier.\n\nActually, looking at the table from the raw output:\n```\n<tr><td rowspan=\"12\">1976</td>...\n```\n\nSo the first column (Final maturity) has \"1976\" for the first row, which spans 12 rows. This means all those securities have a final maturity of 1976. But wait, that's confusing because we see securities maturing in 1972 in that same block...\n\nOh! I think I understand now. The table is showing:\n- The \"Final maturity\" is the year the security will fully mature (its final redemption date)\n- The \"Year and month of final maturity\" column (in the right section) shows when those specific securities will mature\n\nSo the table shows securities that are outstanding as of Feb 1972, and groups them by their final maturity date. Within each group, we see the individual securities that will mature in various years.\n\nBut actually, I think I'm overcomplicating this. Looking at the first table section:\n- Final maturity 1976: Total shows 14,731 (this is for securities that will finally mature in 1976)\n- Then the description shows individual securities that will mature in 1972!\n\nAh! So the description column is showing securities that will mature before the final maturity year. So for the row with Final maturity = 1976, the securities listed are maturing in 1972!\n\nThat's why we see:\n- 1-1/2% Note - 4/1/72-EA (matures April 1, 1972)\n\n\n- 4.558% Bill - 4/21/72 (matures April 21, 1972)\n- etc.\n\nI now understand the table's layout. The first section displays securities grouped by their final maturity date, with the descriptions listing individual securities maturing in earlier years. For the 1976 final maturity group, all securities are actually maturing in 1972. I can see the pattern emerging in the 1972 data - securities are organized by their final maturity year, with the total values showing the aggregate for that year's final maturities. The key is matching the final maturity year to its corresponding total.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_1efa990c063c42beb9272753","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\n# Looking at the raw data more carefully, I need to understand the table structure\n# Let me extract the year-Total pairs by looking at the HTML pattern\n\n# From the 1972 file, I can see the structure is:\n# <tr><td rowspan=\"X\">YEAR</td>...<td>Total</td><td></td><td>NUMBER</td>\n\nimport re\n\nwith open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1972_03_page_45.txt', 'r') as f:\n    content = f.read()\n\n# Find pattern where year is in a rowspan td, followed by Total and a number\n# Pattern: <td>YEAR</td> or <td rowspan=\"X\">YEAR</td> ... then later Total and number\n\n# Let me split by rows and parse\nrows = content.split('</tr>')\nfor row in rows:\n    # Look for year in first column\n    year_match = re.search(r'<td[^>]*>(\\d{4})</td>', row)\n    # Look for Total with number\n    total_match = re.search(r'<td>Total</td><td></td><td>([0-9,]+)</td>', row)\n    if year_match and total_match:\n        print(f\"Year: {year_match.group(1)}, Total: {total_match.group(1)}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
