g at:\n\"1976</td><td>6-1/4% Note - 2/15/76-A</td><td>3,739</td>...\"\n\nAnd at the end of that row, there's \"Total</td><td></td><td>14,731</td>\"\n\nActually, let me re-read the original text file more carefully. Looking at the table:\n- First section shows securities with final maturity in 1976, with amounts for securities maturing in 1972\n- Second section shows securities with final maturity in 1976 on the right side\n\nThe structure is confusing. Let me just manually identify the values from each file:\n\nFrom 1972 file (outstanding Feb 29, 1972):\n- Looking at the table, I can see for each year, there's a \"Total\" row\n\nActually, the simplest approach is to look at the years in the table and match them with totals. Looking at the extracted totals from 1972: 24,221, 22,764, 32,429, 24,785 for 1972-1975 respectively.\n\nI notice these totals align with the expected maturities for each year. The 1976 total appears to be 14,731, which matches the final year's maturity. Each total seems to correspond to the specific year's scheduled securities maturation.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_94293b8f568f42baa5b565da","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\n# I need to understand the table structure better\n# Let me look at where years appear in the first column\n\nwith open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1972_03_page_45.txt', 'r') as f:\n    content = f.read()\n\n# Split into rows and examine\nimport re\nrows = content.split('</tr>')\n\n# Find rows with year labels in first column\nfor i, row in enumerate(rows):\n    # Look for year in the row (4-digit year)\n    year_match = re.search(r'<td[^>]*>(\\d{4})</td>', row)\n    if year_match:\n        year = year_match.group(1)\n        # Look for Total in this row\n        if 'Total' in row:\n            # Find all numbers\n            nums = re.findall(r'<td>([0-9,]+)</td>', row)\n            print(f\"Row {i}: Year={year}, Numbers: {nums}\")\